Nov 11 12:36 postdraft_references_provenance.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    5699 Nov 11 12:27 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    5173 Nov 11 12:27 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   72386 Nov 11 12:27 reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   23625 Nov 11 12:26 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 12:27 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     280 Nov 11 12:27 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    2925 Nov 11 12:28 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    7461 Nov 11 12:45 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    7461 Nov 11 12:43 references.pre_postdraft.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   22559 Nov 11 12:43 references.pre_postdraft.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   36025 Nov 11 12:45 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      16 Nov 11 12:26 results -> ../investigation\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 12:42 scientific-evidence\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 12:41 scripts\ndrwxr-xr-x 7 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 12:41 source_evidence\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 12:31 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     272 Nov 11 12:31 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent       7 Nov 11 12:26 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026, single column, 8–9 body pages)\n\n**Status:** ready. **Provisional title:** How Far Do k Benchmarks Go? Cold-Start Benchmark Selection and Score Imputation on Family-Disjoint Held-Out Models.\n\n## Central question\nWhen only k benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector-plus-imputer recover its scores on the remaining benchmarks, how does the error fall with k, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which k to run?\n\n## Central thesis\nOn MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite with family-disjoint held-out models, a wrapper-selected, rank-Gauss-transformed, NNLS-stacked Gaussian imputer substantially reduces error versus column-mean imputation (sealed final: outcome **qualified**, skill 0.434, pooled nRMSE 0.562) and beats permuted-observation controls clearly (gap 0.678). Its edge over random-k selection with a ridge imputer is small (0.016), MTEB error falls with diminishing returns in k (0.787 at k=2 → 0.638 at k=20), and difficulty varies by leaderboard.\n\n## Why this framing\n- Final evaluation completed and qualified (`final.qualification.*`); a positive-but-modest, calibrated result is supportable. Qualification says nothing beyond the three leaderboards.\n- No `downstream_study`; ablations empty; offline experiment/theory scripts have no reported numbers; candidate diagnostics \"unverified\". So no selector-vs-imputer attribution, no theory claim, no generalization claim.\n- Thesis-first organization: question → protocol → method → error vs k → baselines/controls → per-leaderboard difficulty → limitations. Rejected alternatives: a \"search campaign\" narrative (chronology, three scored variants, one null) and a \"method beats baselines\" headline (overclaims given 0.016 over random-k).\n- Not edit mode; no prior paper.\n\n## Method groups\n1. **Proposed** – selected configuration (best search score, c02; c03 ties exactly): rank-Gauss/z representation, EM Gaussian with missing data, selection by covariance-trace greedy or K-fold held-out-error wrapper (history-only CV), per-target NNLS stack (Gaussian conditional means, ridge, kNN, median), clipping, fallback.\n2. **Variant (ablation-like, confounded)** – greedy conditional-variance + Gaussian conditional mean (search nRMSE 1.067 vs 1.028; skill vs random 0.100 vs 0.132; permuted gap 0.522 vs 0.568). Many components change at once; label as variant comparison.\n3. **Null-result variant** – monotone latent-skill member + A-optimal latent selector: identical search metrics to the selected configuration; cause unknown.\n4. **Baselines/controls** – column-mean, random-k ridge, greedy-forward ridge, permuted and constant controls (aggregated values only).\n\n## Results focus\n- Error-vs-k on MTEB (4 points) as the answer to \"how large a k\"; no sufficiency threshold claimed.\n- Per-leaderboard nRMSE: MTEB 0.703, HELM 0.501, OLL 0.482; k grids and column counts differ (OLL has 6 columns), so not a pure difficulty ranking.\n- Skill: vs column-mean 0.434 (seed episodes 0.433/0.427/0.433), vs greedy ridge 0.099, vs random-k ridge 0.016; selector and imputer are scored jointly.\n- Permuted-control gap 0.678.\n- Search-split three-variant table (two seeds each), kept separate from sealed results.\n- Limitations: joint scoring, three seeds, no CIs on pooled metrics, embedding-model leaderboard, non-adaptive selection, missing ablations/theory.\n\n## Figures (both full-width; ICLR text width ≈5.5 in, no column layout to rescue dense figures)\n- **protocol_overview** (`generate-concept-figure`, page 1–2, 1 panel): history matrix → commit k → observe → impute → score; names leaderboards and baselines.\n- **main_results** (matplotlib, 3 panels, PDF + 300-DPI PNG, PDF in LaTeX): (a) MTEB nRMSE vs k; (b) nRMSE by leaderboard + pooled; (c) skill vs three baselines. No invented error bars. No progression views.\n- Tables: compact final-metric table; search-variant table.\n\n## Body-page use\nProblem formulation and protocol (including metric definitions), reimplementable method, results (figure + two tables), substantive limitations and practitioner discussion. Tighten rather than pad if evidence is short.\n\n## Supplement plan\n1. Evaluator protocol and data partitions (pinned revisions, sizes, split rule, k grids, seeds, controls, aggregation hierarchy).\n2. Full method specification and hyperparameters (incl. latent-skill null-result extension).\n3. Full final-metric table, seed episodes, search-variant metrics.\n4. Unperformed analyses, data/licence notes, LLM Usage statement.\n\n## Forbidden headings/phrases\nExperiment 1/2, iteration history, search campaign, progression, first successful run, milestones, run log, cycle-by-cycle; \"correctness rate\", \"correct and eligible\", \"combined_score\", \"rubric\", \"state of the art\", \"significantly better\" (no tests), \"first to\".\n\n## Writer cautions\nNever invent per-baseline nRMSE, per-family skill, CIs, ablation or theory numbers. Only cite bib entries that are relevant (SubLIME, SimBA, From Benchmarks to Skills, Mapping Overlaps); the bib is otherwise mostly irrelevant retrieval noise and lacks the starter-literature papers. Search nRMSE (>1) and final nRMSE (<1) are different partitions; do not compare them. Double-blind and LLM Usage section required.\nfigures:\nmain_results.pdf\nmain_results.pdf.prov.json\nmain_results.png\nmain_results.png.prov.json\nprotocol_overview.png\nprotocol_overview.prompts.json\n\nscientific-evidence:\nbundle.manifest.json\nledger.jsonl\nschemas\nspec.md\nverification-report.json\nls: cannot access 'validation_feedback*': No such file or directory\nls: cannot access 'paper_review_feedback*': No such file or directory\nls: cannot access 'posthoc_ranking.json': No such file or directory\ndraft_reference_gaps.json\ndraft_reference_gaps.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\ndraft_reference_gaps_prompt.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.json\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.md\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ee44c22-3ef6-4a15-a34d-6d93973a8a38","uuid":"835b7977-3411-4ab5-8bc1-1dc4e04cc34a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:45:49.831Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 4088\ndrwx------ 9 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 12:45 .\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 12:26 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     751 Nov 11 12:26 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 12:26 .claude\n-rw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 12:26 .paper_two_stage_enabled\nprw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 12:45 .postdraft_citation_weave_stream_20261111_122649.fifo\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    3685 Nov 11 12:45 VENUE.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 agent agent 