e,\n  title = {{Inference and Missing Data}},\n@article{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign,\n  title = {{Bayesian Experimental Design: A Review}},\n@book{Embretson2000IRT,\n  title = {{Item Response Theory for Psychologists}},\n@inproceedings{Vivek2024AnchorPoints,\n  title = {{Anchor Points: Benchmarking Models with Much Fewer Examples}},\n  booktitle = {Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics}\n@inproceedings{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,\n  title = {{Observational Scaling Laws and the Predictability of Language Model Performance}},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems}\n@article{Hoerl1970Ridge,\n  title = {{Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems}},\n@book{Lawson1995NNLS,\n  title = {{Solving Least Squares Problems}},\n@inproceedings{Krause2007ObservationSelection,\n  title = {{Near-optimal observation selection using submodular functions}},\n  booktitle = {AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence},\n@inproceedings{Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking,\n  title = {{Efficient Benchmarking (of Language Models)}},\n  booktitle = {Findings of NAACL-HLT},\n@article{Srivastava2023BIGbench,\n  title = {{Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models}},\n@inproceedings{Vania2021ComparingTestSets,\n  title = {{Comparing Test Sets with Item Response Theory}},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of ACL-IJCNLP},\nanalysis:\nprotocol_facts.json\nsearch_metrics.json\n\nscripts:\n_fetched_refs.json\n_fetched_refs2.json\n_references_orig.bib\n_references_provenance_orig.json\nadd_refs.py\nadd_refs2.py\nbuild_figures.py\nextract_protocol_facts.py\nextract_search_metrics.py","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ee44c22-3ef6-4a15-a34d-6d93973a8a38","uuid":"73b9557b-e069-47ba-9592-363f470a9ed4","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:45:52.999Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is well supported overall: the methods (EM, stacking, NNLS, submodular selection, ridge), item-level cheaper-evaluation work, and latent-capability work are all cited, and the quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`. Three targeted gaps remain. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **related-work-comparators** (Related Work, high, comparator)\n   - Anchor: \"Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion ... are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices\"; \"here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark\".\n   - The closest prior work predicts unrun benchmark scores from a few observed ones, applies low-rank or matrix-completion methods to LLM score matrices, or selects what to run adaptively. The draft cites only generic matrix-completion sources and item-level work. The paper's differentiation claim is therefore not positioned against its direct competitors.\n   - Search topics: predicting LLM performance on unseen benchmarks from a benchmark subset; matrix completion or low-rank factorization of model-by-benchmark matrices; adaptive or IRT-based active selection for LLM evaluation.\n\n2. **intro-benchmark-redundancy-claim** (Introduction, medium, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"are known to overlap in what they measure\".\n   - The premise that motivates budgeted evaluation is cited only to the suites themselves, which do not document redundancy.\n   - Search topics: correlation or redundancy among LLM benchmarks; low-dimensional structure in benchmark scores.\n\n3. **data-open-llm-leaderboard-context** (Method, protocol data paragraph, medium, benchmark_context)\n   - Anchor: \"the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models\".\n   - The leaderboard is one of three sealed evaluation datasets, and its six component benchmarks are named without citations. HELM and MTEB are cited.\n   - Search topics: Open LLM Leaderboard v2 description; IFEval, BBH, GPQA, MuSR and MMLU-Pro papers.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Cross-benchmark score prediction and matrix completion on LLM evaluation matrices\n- Adaptive or IRT-based test and benchmark selection for LLM evaluation\n- Open LLM Leaderboard and its component benchmarks\n- Empirical evidence of benchmark redundancy\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Rank-Gauss transform and kNN: standard components, and the draft already states them as such.\n- Numeric results and protocol facts: grounded in evidence files.\n- Limitations and discussion text: deliberately self-critical, with no external claims that need support.\n# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. The gap artifact reports three targeted gaps, so this plan has six queries and no broader refresh. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n**Central topic:** choosing k benchmarks to run on a new model and imputing its scores on the remaining benchmarks from a historical model-by-benchmark matrix (MTEB, HELM Lite, Open LLM Leaderboard).\n\n## Existing bibliography (not re-searched)\nThe bibliography already covers tinyBenchmarks, metabench, anchor points, efficient benchmarking, Sloth, observational scaling laws and BenchBench. It also covers EM, stacking, NNLS, ridge, submodular selection, Bayesian design, IRT (Embretson, Vania), MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB. None of these directions is repeated below.\n\n## Queries\n\n### Gap 1: related-work-comparators (high priority)\nRelated Work claims \"the unit of selection is a whole benchmark\" but is not positioned against benchmark-level competitors. The three queries cover the three directions named in the gap memo.\n1. **baseline_comparator:** \"predicting language model performance on unseen benchmarks from scores on a subset of benchmarks\". This targets direct competitors that predict unrun benchmark scores from a few observed ones.\n2. **baseline_comparator:** \"matrix completion low-rank factorization of model-by-benchmark score matrix for LLM evaluation\". The draft cites only generic matrix-completion sources (Candes, Salakhutdinov), so this looks for applications to LLM score matrices.\n3. **baseline_comparator:** \"adaptive testing item response theory active selection of evaluations for large language models\". This targets adaptive or IRT-based selection of what to run, beyond the textbook and item-reduction work already cited.\n\n### Gap 2: intro-benchmark-redundancy-claim (medium)\n4. **result_driven_theme:** \"redundancy and correlation among LLM benchmarks low-dimensional structure of benchmark scores\". The claim that suites \"are known to overlap in what they measure\" is cited only to the suites. This looks for empirical redundancy or correlation analyses beyond BenchBench and Observational Scaling Laws, which are already cited.\n\n### Gap 3: data-open-llm-leaderboard-context (medium)\n5. **benchmark_context:** \"Open LLM Leaderboard v2 evaluation of open language models\". This finds a source for one of the three sealed evaluation datasets.\n6. **benchmark_context:** \"IFEval instruction-following, BIG-Bench Hard, GPQA, MuSR, MMLU-Pro benchmark\". This finds sources for the six component benchmarks named in the protocol and appendix. BBH may already be partly covered by BIG-bench, and MATH Level 5 is not queried because the gap memo does not list it as a search topic. The retrieval step can resolve the individual papers.\n\n## Exclusions\nThe gap memo deliberately does not flag the rank-Gauss transform, kNN, numeric results, protocol facts, or the limitations and discussion text. No queries are planned for these. Queries must not name or hint at the prior published paper on this exact task.\n[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 6 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/6 | predicting language model performance on unseen benchmarks from scores on a subset of benchmarks\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/25 (25 left) | candidates 0/0 | query 1/6\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 2/6 | matrix completion low-