{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sc7Kb77J8MqDqkco4LqP35","type":"tool_result","content":"39:\\section{Introduction}\n40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n65:\\section{Related Work}\n68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n74:\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n76:\\subsection{Problem setup and success criterion}\\label{sec:setup}\n87:\\subsection{Design rationale}\\label{sec:rationale}\n94:\\subsection{Compared approaches}\\label{sec:variants}\n97:\\subsection{Selector and imputer}\\label{sec:impl}\n106:\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\\label{sec:protocol}\n107:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n115:\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n124:\\subsection{Main results}\n150:\\subsection{Error against the budget $k$}\n154:\\subsection{Baselines and controls}\n161:\\subsection{Difficulty across leaderboards}\n164:\\subsection{Reading the metrics: sensitivity and failure analysis}\n169:\\subsection{Configuration comparison on the model-development partition}\\label{sec:search}\n192:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n207:\\section{Conclusion}\n216:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n218:\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\n241:\\subsection{Method specification and hyperparameters}\n271:\\subsection{Extra evidence tables}\n289:\\subsection{Unperformed analyses}\n292:\\subsection{Reproducibility, data and licences}\n295:\\subsection{LLM Usage}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ee44c22-3ef6-4a15-a34d-6d93973a8a38","uuid":"eda790d7-6ed0-486b-b522-bb273d712af4","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:46:07.892Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"39:\\section{Introduction}\n40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n65:\\section{Related Work}\n68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n74:\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n76:\\subsection{Problem setup and success criterion}\\label{sec:setup}\n87:\\subsection{Design rationale}\\label{sec:rationale}\n94:\\subsection{Compared approaches}\\label{sec:variants}\n97:\\subsection{Selector and imputer}\\label{sec:impl}\n106:\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\\label{sec:protocol}\n107:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n115:\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n124:\\subsection{Main results}\n150:\\subsection{Error against the budget $k$}\n154:\\subsection{Baselines and controls}\n161:\\subsection{Difficulty across leaderboards}\n164:\\subsection{Reading the metrics: sensitivity and failure analysis}\n169:\\subsection{Configuration comparison on the model-development partition}\\label{sec:search}\n192:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n207:\\section{Conclusion}\n216:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n218:\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\n241:\\subsection{Method specification and hyperparameters}\n271:\\subsection{Extra evidence tables}\n289:\\subsection{Unperformed analyses}\n292:\\subsection{Reproducibility, data and licences}\n295:\\subsection{LLM Usage}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
