{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0111gwLUiSSj6aT7s3gvVbyb","type":"tool_result","content":"\\section{Introduction}\nA full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n\nPrior work on cheaper evaluation mostly reduces the number of \\emph{items} within one benchmark \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}, distills a handful of benchmarks into a sparse one \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}, or fits low-dimensional capability models across many benchmarks \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. The setting studied here differs in three ways: the selection is committed \\emph{before} any score of the new model is seen, the target is the score on every unrun benchmark, and the new model comes from a family that is absent from the history.\n\n\\paragraph{Research question and hypotheses.} When only $k$ benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector--imputer pair recover its other scores, how does the error fall with $k$, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which $k$ to run? We examine four hypotheses.\n\\textbf{H1}: observed scores on a selected subset predict the remaining scores better than history-only column means.\n\\textbf{H2}: the error decreases with $k$ with diminishing returns.\n\\textbf{H3}: a model-based selector adds accuracy beyond random selection under a comparable imputer.\n\\textbf{H4}: difficulty differs across leaderboards.\n\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Using a sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models from three leaderboards, H1 and H2 are supported, H4 is observed but confounded by the number of benchmarks per leaderboard, and H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-$k$ ridge). Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) A precise cold-start protocol with committed selection, family-disjoint held-out models, evaluator-owned baselines and negative controls, and a hierarchical aggregation rule (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).\n(ii) A reimplementable selector--imputer pair built from standard components: rank-Gauss transform, EM Gaussian, wrapper selection, NNLS stacking (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n(iii) An error-versus-$k$ curve on MTEB and per-leaderboard errors, with skill relative to three baselines and a permutation control (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n(iv) A calibrated account of what the evidence cannot show: no ablation, no theory check, joint scoring of selector and imputer, and seed variation that does not reflect held-out sampling (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Cold-start protocol. A history matrix with missing cells is given to the method; it commits to $k$ benchmark indices; only the new model's scores on those $k$ benchmarks are revealed; the method predicts all other benchmarks; error is measured in units of history column standard deviations and compared with column-mean, random-$k$ ridge and greedy-forward ridge baselines and with permuted-observation controls.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Benchmark suites and their overlap.} Multi-task suites aggregate many sub-benchmarks \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB,Zhuo2024BigCodeBench}, and judge-based protocols add conversational evaluations \\citep{Zheng2023MTBench}. \\citet{Perlitz2024BenchBench} show that conclusions about benchmark agreement depend strongly on methodological choices such as the reference set and the correlation measure; we therefore define the target, the held-out split and the aggregation explicitly rather than relying on a correlation summary.\n\n\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\n\n\\paragraph{Latent-capability and scaling views.} Observational scaling laws and skill scaling laws explain multi-benchmark performance through a few latent capability factors estimated from public evaluations \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}, and item response theory has long modelled responses through latent ability \\citep{Embretson2000IRT,Vania2021ComparingTestSets}. Our imputer uses a Gaussian dependence structure rather than an explicit latent-factor law, and we evaluate on family-disjoint models rather than within-family extrapolation.\n\n\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n\n\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\n36:Evaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\ti\n66:\\paragraph{Benchmark suites and their overlap.} Multi-task suites aggregate many sub-benchmarks \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB,Zhuo2024BigCodeBench}, and judge-based \n70:\\paragraph{Latent-capability and scaling views.} Observational scaling laws and skill scaling laws explain multi-benchmark performance through a few latent capability factors estimated from public evaluations \\citep{R\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and \n107:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 h\n109:\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus evaluator-owned controls. In a \\emph{permuted} control, held-\n120:\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (OLL: Open LLM Leaderboard) and pooled over the three. (c) Ski\n139:\\quad MTEB / HELM / Open LLM & 0.703 / 0.501 / 0.482 & per leaderboard\\\\\n152:The mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differences of the four tabulated means divided by the number of ad\n162:$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. It is not a clean difficulty ranking: the leaderboards differ\n233:Open LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\n239:\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; O\n290:The following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling inter\n293:Entry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{predict}); dependencies are NumPy only; behaviour is determin","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ee44c22-3ef6-4a15-a34d-6d93973a8a38","uuid":"81c32d74-5fd4-4901-9cf8-5a80975468b2","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:47:18.568Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\section{Introduction}\nA full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n\nPrior work on cheaper evaluation mostly reduces the number of \\emph{items} within one benchmark \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}, distills a handful of benchmarks into a sparse one \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}, or fits low-dimensional capability models across many benchmarks \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. The setting studied here differs in three ways: the selection is committed \\emph{before} any score of the new model is seen, the target is the score on every unrun benchmark, and the new model comes from a family that is absent from the history.\n\n\\paragraph{Research question and hypotheses.} When only $k$ benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector--imputer pair recover its other scores, how does the error fall with $k$, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which $k$ to run? We examine four hypotheses.\n\\textbf{H1}: observed scores on a selected subset predict the remaining scores better than history-only column means.\n\\textbf{H2}: the error decreases with $k$ with diminishing returns.\n\\textbf{H3}: a model-based selector adds accuracy beyond random selection under a comparable imputer.\n\\textbf{H4}: difficulty differs across leaderboards.\n\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Using a sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models from three leaderboards, H1 and H2 are supported, H4 is observed but confounded by the number of benchmarks per leaderboard, and H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-$k$ ridge). Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) A precise cold-start protocol with committed selection, family-disjoint held-out models, evaluator-owned baselines and negative controls, and a hierarchical aggregation rule (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).\n(ii) A reimplementable selector--imputer pair built from standard components: rank-Gauss transform, EM Gaussian, wrapper selection, NNLS stacking (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n(iii) An error-versus-$k$ curve on MTEB and per-leaderboard errors, with skill relative to three baselines and a permutation control (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n(iv) A calibrated account of what the evidence cannot show: no ablation, no theory check, joint scoring of selector and imputer, and seed variation that does not reflect held-out sampling (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Cold-start protocol. A history matrix with missing cells is given to the method; it commits to $k$ benchmark indices; only the new model's scores on those $k$ benchmarks are revealed; the method predicts all other benchmarks; error is measured in units of history column standard deviations and compared with column-mean, random-$k$ ridge and greedy-forward ridge baselines and with permuted-observation controls.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Benchmark suites and their overlap.} Multi-task suites aggregate many sub-benchmarks \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB,Zhuo2024BigCodeBench}, and judge-based protocols add conversational evaluations \\citep{Zheng2023MTBench}. \\citet{Perlitz2024BenchBench} show that conclusions about benchmark agreement depend strongly on methodological choices such as the reference set and the correlation measure; we therefore define the target, the held-out split and the aggregation explicitly rather than relying on a correlation summary.\n\n\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\n\n\\paragraph{Latent-capability and scaling views.} Observational scaling laws and skill scaling laws explain multi-benchmark performance through a few latent capability factors estimated from public evaluations \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}, and item response theory has long modelled responses through latent ability \\citep{Embretson2000IRT,Vania2021ComparingTestSets}. Our imputer uses a Gaussian dependence structure rather than an explicit latent-factor law, and we evaluate on family-disjoint models rather than within-family extrapolation.\n\n\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n\n\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\n36:Evaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\ti\n66:\\paragraph{Benchmark suites and their overlap.} Multi-task suites aggregate many sub-benchmarks \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB,Zhuo2024BigCodeBench}, and judge-based \n70:\\paragraph{Latent-capability and scaling views.} Observational scaling laws and skill scaling laws explain multi-benchmark performance through a few latent capability factors estimated from public evaluations \\citep{R\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and \n107:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 h\n109:\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus evaluator-owned controls. In a \\emph{permuted} control, held-\n120:\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (OLL: Open LLM Leaderboard) and pooled over the three. (c) Ski\n139:\\quad MTEB / HELM / Open LLM & 0.703 / 0.501 / 0.482 & per leaderboard\\\\\n152:The mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differences of the four tabulated means divided by th