{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwAboS4FU1B19JgruBUu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XzMNoFEWnEFo36pTkpkRW4","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('references.bib').read()\ns=s.replace(\"Ed H. and\",\"Ed H. Chi and\").replace(\"and Wei, Jason}\",\"and Jason Wei}\").replace(\"Wang Chun\",\"Chun Wang\")\nopen('references.bib','w').write(s)\nt=open('paper.tex').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global t\n    assert t.count(a)==1,a[:40]\n    t=t.replace(a,b)\nrep(r\"are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests\",\n    r\"are known to overlap in what they measure: agreement analyses across dozens of benchmarks and low-dimensional capability models fitted to public evaluations both point to strongly related scores \\citep{Perlitz2024BenchBench,Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. This suggests\")\nrep(r\"\\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB,Zhuo2024BigCodeBench}, and judge-based protocols add conversational evaluations \\citep{Zheng2023MTBench}.\",\n    r\"\\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB}.\")\nrep(r\"These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\",\n    r\"Fluid benchmarking goes further by choosing items adaptively under an item-response model of latent ability \\citep{Hofmann2025Fluid}. These works reduce or adapt item counts within a benchmark or a fixed suite; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark, the choice is committed before any score of the new model is seen, and the target is every benchmark not run.\")\nrep(r\"and item response theory has long modelled responses through latent ability \\citep{Embretson2000IRT,Vania2021ComparingTestSets}.\",\n    r\"and item response theory has long modelled responses through latent ability \\citep{Embretson2000IRT}. These latent-factor predictors are the closest benchmark-level relatives of the present setting; we did not run them as baselines, so how they compare under our protocol is left open.\")\nrep(r\"\\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}\",r\"\\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}\")\nrep(r\"the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371\",\n    r\"the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks, namely IFEval \\citep{Zhou2023IFEval}, BBH \\citep{Suzgun2023BBH}, MATH Level~5 \\citep{Hendrycks2021MATH}, GPQA \\citep{Rein2023GPQA}, MuSR \\citep{Sprague2023MuSR} and MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024MMLUPro}; 3371\")\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(t)\nE\ngrep -n \"Open LLM Leaderboard\" paper.tex | cut -c1-160 | sed -n 1,12p; sed -n 192,206p paper.tex | cut -c1-400"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":996,"cache_read_input_tokens":80135,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":996},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ee44c22-3ef6-4a15-a34d-6d93973a8a38","uuid":"d4e06dea-1a3c-4025-8316-94e6038728cb","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:48:02.347Z","request_id":"req_011CgwAbnefcsa13eofSmBoC"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XzMNoFEWnEFo36pTkpkRW4","type":"tool_result","content":"36:Evaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We stu\n107:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out mod\n109:\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus e\n120:\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (O\n152:The mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differe\n162:$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. I\n233:Open LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\n239:\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken f\n290:The following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace obje\n293:Entry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{p\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column means, and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations \n\n\\paragraph{Joint scoring and missing ablations.} Selector and imputer are scored together. No controlled experiment varied one while holding the other fixed, so the contributions of the rank-Gauss transform, the wrapper selector, the stack and the clipping are unattributed. The development-partition comparison changes many components at once. A fair selector test would pair each selector with each\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} Reported values are seed means. The seeds vary only the method's internal randomness (fold assignments), not the held-out models, baselines or controls, so the 0.006 spread across seeds understates sampling uncertainty over models. Held-out sets are small for MTEB (37 models) and HELM Lite (25), and the held-out groups are few (25 and 7). The 0.016 margin over random-$k$ s\n\n\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name and content heuristics. Selection is non-adaptive; adaptive stopping or sequential selection could change the picture. Missingness in leade\n\n\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (1) \\emph{Leakage through naming.} Family-disjointness relies on name normalisation, a family key and a content hash; a model re-released under an unrecognised name could cross the split. (2) \\emph{Metric scale.} Errors are in history standard deviations of each benchmark, which makes a benchmark with a narrow history spread (e.g.\\ one saturated by strong models) l\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ee44c22-3ef6-4a15-a34d-6d93973a8a38","uuid":"c0e2e141-bbef-45c3-83bf-37bdbe6c15f4","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:48:02.436Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"36:Evaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We stu\n107:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out mod\n109:\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus e\n120:\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (O\n152:The mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differe\n162:$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. I\n233:Open LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\n239:\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken f\n290:The following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace obje\n293:Entry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{p\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column means, and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations \n\n\\paragraph{Joint scoring and missing ablations.} Selector and imputer are scored together. No controlled experiment varied one while holding the other fixed, so the contributions of the rank-Gauss transform, the wrapper selector, the stack and the clipping are unattributed. The development-partition comparison changes many components at once. A fair selector test would pair each selector with each\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} Reported values are seed means. The seeds vary only the method's internal randomness (fold assignments), not the held-out models, baselines or controls, so the 0.006 spread across seeds understates sampling uncertainty over models. Held-out sets are small for MTEB (37 models) and HELM Lite (25), and the held-out groups are few (25 and 7). The 0.016 margin over random-$k$ s\n\n\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name and content heuristics. Selection is non-adaptive; adaptive stopping or sequential selection could change the picture. Missingness in leade\n\n\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (1) \\emph{Leakage through naming.} Family-disjointness relies on name normalisation, a family key and a content hash; a model re-released under an unrecognised name could cross the split. (2) \\emph{Metric scale.} Errors are in history standard deviations of each benchmark, which makes a benchmark with a narrow history spread (e.g.\\ one saturated by strong models) l\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
